R(1,'field-compass','field-current-demonstrator','field-current-deflection','Current-deflection record','The compass changes direction when current flows in the nearby wire. The needle is not wired into the circuit: the observation links electric current with a magnetic effect.',['field-oersted-object','field-oersted-observation'])
R(2,'field-current-deflection','field-position-cards','field-around-wire','Around-the-wire comparison','The recorded needles above and below the same wire point in opposite directions. A simple pull straight toward the wire does not describe this pattern.',['field-oersted-observation'],['field-current-deflection','field-position-cards'])
R(3,'field-bar-magnet','field-compass','field-direction-map','Magnetic-direction map','Compass directions at several positions trace a curved pattern around the bar magnet. The arrows represent local direction; they are not visible threads or a measured map of strength.',['field-teaching'])
R(4,'field-direction-map','field-around-wire','field-comparison','Field comparison sheet','The magnet and the current-carrying wire both organize compass directions in the space around them, but the patterns differ. Field thinking records what a test needle does at each position, rather than merely naming an attraction.',['field-teaching','field-oersted-observation'],['field-direction-map','field-around-wire'])
R(5,'field-coil-meter','field-bar-magnet','field-motion-record','Moving-and-still record','In the supplied coil trials the needle deflects while the magnet approaches, returns to zero when it rests, and deflects the other way as it withdraws. A magnet present beside the coil is not enough for a sustained reading.',['field-teaching'])
R(6,'field-motion-record','experimental-protocol','field-repeatable-comparison','Repeatable induction comparison','The protocol preserves the same coil, magnet orientation and meter while distinguishing approach, rest and withdrawal. Repeated recorded trials retain the zero reading at rest rather than discarding it as an uninteresting failure.',['field-teaching'],['field-motion-record'])
R(7,'field-ring-replica','field-switch-log','field-transient-record','Ring transient record','The second winding gives a brief response at connection and disconnection, not a sustained response while the first current stays steady. Neither winding has to move. Faraday’s surviving 1831 ring anchors this reconstructed comparison.',['field-ring-object','field-teaching'],['field-switch-log'])
R(8,'field-repeatable-comparison','field-transient-record','field-change-argument','Change-not-presence argument','The moving magnet and the stationary ring support the same distinction: changing magnetic linkage, not mere magnetic presence, induces the response. Mechanical motion is one way to obtain the change, not a requirement in every arrangement.',['field-teaching','field-ring-object'],['field-repeatable-comparison','field-transient-record'])
R(9,'field-change-argument','field-comparison','field-induction-model','Field-and-induction model','The model connects a spatial magnetic pattern to a changing linkage through a coil. It explains the contrasting observations without claiming that the compass map alone proves induction, or that one diagram supplies a complete electromagnetic theory.',['field-teaching'],['field-change-argument','field-comparison'])
R(10,'field-induction-model','field-hand-generator','field-explained-exhibit','Explained generator exhibit','The handle keeps changing magnetic linkage through the generator’s coil, and the recorded lamp responds while it turns. The handle supplies mechanical energy: the magnet is not a fuel. The exhibit now connects an everyday generator with the earlier contrast between steady presence and change.',['field-generator','field-teaching'],['field-induction-model'])
